R=[scoped_record('c0823','dancers-endtime-ring-provision','The Dancers at the End of Time — Ring-supported humanity','The Dancers at the End of Time','The established matter-altering ring society',['book','short_story'],
 'End of Time series setting, before terminal ring failure','The continuing ring-supported era before the Lost Cities’ energy depletion','Living human and human-derived End of Time residents',
 ['The final ring failure as unchanged provision','Any magical-looking action as unsupported nontechnical fantasy','Dead or vanished people as leisure beneficiaries'],
 'End of Time residents use personal power rings to reshape matter and create their surroundings from ancient stored energy. The supporting Lost Cities make this a technological inheritance rather than an unexplained ability to conjure necessities. Within the functioning system, residents can obtain material conditions through their rings instead of ordinary subsistence employment. Guarantees are rated strong, with the later exhaustion demonstrating a real limit to that apparent abundance.\n\nPractical productive access is widely dispersed among ring users, supporting a distributed human-control estimate. The gathered account does not establish equal control over every part of the ancient energy infrastructure, so the interval remains broad. Appearance, immortality and fantastic scale do not make the users external AI. Their culture is heavily recreational, but constructing experiences, creating art, teaching and caring can all be useful work. Nonworking therefore remains unresolved. The point covers the established supported era rather than the catastrophe or its special time-travel resolution, and does not multiply every time-displaced appearance into a new person.',
 [-.75,.75,None],[[-1,-.15],[.3,1],None],
 ['Personal rings alter matter using actual inherited energy infrastructure; their later failure marks a distinct limit rather than absence of earlier support.','Widely distributed personal productive access supports human control, while ownership of the underlying Lost Cities remains uncertain.',nw],refs,2,proxy=True)]
