R.append(scoped_record('c0824','mockingbird-robot-maintained-humans','Mockingbird — Robot-maintained New York','Mockingbird','The declining human city supported by automatic services',['book'],
 'Walter Tevis’s novel-specific future','Established mechanical support before the protagonists’ later social changes','Living human New York residents under the robot service system',
 ['A uniform verified child census','Decline and death as 100 percent leisure','Spofforth’s copied human memories as unambiguously external AI'],
 'Automatic stores dispense food and clothing, while robots maintain the routines on which the remaining population depends. This is actual continuing material support amid institutional decline. Guarantees receive a cautiously favorable estimate because ordinary provision persists, while malfunction, isolation and reproductive damage undermine security. The estimate does not confuse pleasant sedation with the satisfaction of every human need.\n\nEffective control remains unresolved. Robotic administration is clear, but the most important surviving controller, Spofforth, contains edited human memories; the exact relation between that human-derived personality, other machines and inherited rules is not reduced to a simple external-AI coordinate. The AI-provision regime remains provisional. Much ordinary service work is delegated, yet teaching, reading, care and creative activity remain useful human work. No all-person census follows from drug use or a shrinking population, so nonworking stays null. Conflicting exact demographic details in short summaries are excluded, and dead people are never counted as liberated nonworkers.',
 [-.45,None,None],[[-.85,.55],None,None],
 ['The identified automatic stores and robot maintenance provide ongoing food/clothing/support, with institutional decay and malfunction qualifying its security.','Robot administration and Spofforth’s edited human-memory origin leave ultimate human-versus-external-AI control unresolved.',nw],refs,5,alternatives=[1]))
refs=[P+'spacer-author',P+'spacer']
R.append(scoped_record('c0829','2312-spacer-needs-economy','2312 — The spacer necessities economy','2312','Cooperative space settlements with quantum-assisted planning',['book'],
 'Kim Stanley Robinson’s 2312 spacer economic model','Established space-settlement economy outside the Terminator attack emergency','Living human spacer residents of the described cooperative network',
 ['All Earth markets as identical','Every terrarium as autonomous AI sovereign property','The author’s real Mondragon or Antarctica examples as literal fictional locations'],
 'The spacer economy supplies necessities on an as-needed, nonmonetary basis while retaining smaller markets for extras. The author explicitly connects this fictional arrangement to shared economic ownership and durable settlement institutions. Quantum computers coordinate the planned economy and its technological habitat network, materially supporting distribution beyond individual wage purchases. Guarantees are strong for the described network, with local differences and frontier conditions left as qualifications.\n\nControl is placed toward shared human ownership because workers and citizens collectively hold the economic system. Computer planning is administration here, not sufficient evidence of autonomous AI sovereignty. The estimate remains broad because the network mixes institutions and the exact powers of planners and local bodies vary. This is not a claim that production requires no people: habitat work, farming, care, transport, research and art remain useful. No all-person participation rate can be derived from free necessities or cooperative organization, so nonworking stays unresolved. Earth’s remaining market economies and specific attack emergencies are outside the scoped baseline.',
 [-.75,.7,None],[[-.95,-.2],[.35,1],None],
 ['The author explicitly specifies as-needed nonmonetary necessities; quantum-assisted economic coordination and technological habitats support the distribution model.','The author describes shared worker/citizen ownership; computer planning is not automatically independent machine sovereignty.',nw],refs,2))
refs=[P+'panga',P+'psalm']
R.append(scoped_record('c0832','monk-robot-backland-automation-refusal','Monk & Robot — Panga’s anti-automation community','A Psalm for the Wild-Built / A Prayer for the Crown-Shy','The specific back-to-the-land community described during the sequel journey',['book'],
 'Monk & Robot duology; the specific community appears in A Prayer for the Crown-Shy','The established community encountered during Dex and Mosscap’s tea-service route','Living human members of the explicitly automation-eschewing community',
 ['All Panga as rejecting automation','The free robot population as forced human workers','General Pangan basics as a verified local transfer charter','Respect for robots as a proven job-protection motive'],
 'One Pangan community deliberately rejects automation because it fears a return to the Factory Age. This is a specific technological choice within a wider society that has experienced robot industry and retains access to advanced alternatives. The community is included as an interpretive zero variant based on ecological and social restraint, not as evidence that all Panga protects employment or abandons technology. Human productive activity remains part of its chosen way of life.\n\nThe short critical account does not specify local productive ownership, decision procedures or how this community obtains every necessity, so guarantees and control remain unresolved. The broader duology’s accessible basics cannot simply be copied into a precise local entitlement or transfer rule. Robots’ decision to leave industrial service also does not turn them into human-owned automation waiting to be reclaimed. Nonworking remains null because philosophical permission to exist without proving usefulness is not an activity census. The source identifies this community in the sequel; the original discovery title is transparently broadened to the duology rather than claiming the scene occurs in the first novella.',
 [None,None,None],[None,None,None],
 ['The critical review explicitly identifies a community that eschews automation to avoid the Factory Age despite known technological alternatives.','The community’s own productive ownership and governance are not specified; a distributed-human analogy remains provisional.',nw],refs,2,zero=True))

